From bdde4db0472a81c939138dd5631bad82cb247cc9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Sebastian Werner <swernerx@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 23 Sep 2026 20:51:15 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] perf(parser): run the fused root scan on x86-64 with SSE2
 and AVX2

The root scan that finds the first NUL and the first `]:` in one pass
existed only for aarch64 (NEON); x86-64 kept the plain NUL `memchr` in
normalization, then the pre-pass's `[` probe and a `]:` `memmem`.

x86-64 now runs the same loop. SSE2, part of the x86-64 baseline,
compares 16-byte blocks for NUL and `]`, with a second load one byte on
for `:`, four blocks to a group sharing one branch and an unsigned
lane-wise minimum for their NUL test. Where the CPU reports AVX2 at run
time, the same loop runs on 32-byte blocks; `is_x86_feature_detected!`
caches its answer, and published builds keep targeting the baseline.
Both end on one vector over the last 16 or 32 bytes, whose lookahead
comes from the `:` lane bits shifted down by one, so a `]` in the final
byte starts no `]:`. The AVX2 loop hands bodies under 32 bytes to SSE2,
and bodies under 16 bytes are walked byte by byte as on aarch64. Other
targets keep the previous searches, and aarch64 code is unchanged.

The root scan's differential tests now also compare the SSE2 loop and,
where detected, the AVX2 loop with the byte-at-a-time oracle, over
lengths up to 200 (tails up to 300, past two AVX2 groups), and add a NUL
at every offset among bytes from 0x80 up.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
