From 2bf6ba561ec571b8d8ac5756c58fc54df59d2432 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Sebastian Werner <swernerx@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 23 Sep 2026 21:37:48 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] perf(parser): keep root scans under 512 bytes on SSE2 on
 x86-64

The first x86-64 revision took the AVX2 loop for every body of 32 bytes
or more. The x86-64 paired harness put the smallest comments below parity
in fresh and reuse parsing (comment-ack, 37 bytes, fresh 0.940-0.961;
comment-reproduction, 298 bytes, reuse 0.913-0.943), while the broad set
parsed at 1.025-1.027.

The AVX2 loop has costs the SSE2 one does not: the feature check, a call
that cannot be inlined across its target_feature boundary, clearing the
upper register halves on return, and a wider tail. Against those it saves
one group iteration per 128 bytes. The dispatch now takes AVX2 only for
bodies of at least four of its groups (512 bytes), and tests the length
first, so a shorter body runs the SSE2 loop without the feature check.
The AVX2 loop still answers for any length, and the tests still compare
it with the oracle at every length.

A new test puts NUL and `]:` at every offset of every length within one
AVX2 vector of the threshold, plus a trailing `]` and no needle.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 src/parser/root_scan.rs | 60 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++------
 1 file changed, 52 insertions(+), 8 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/parser/root_scan.rs b/src/parser/root_scan.rs
index 553dcd86..875e1b76 100644
